#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Run one headless `agy` review and refuse to call it a success unless it really was.

`agy` exits 0 with status=SUCCESS and an empty response when every tool it wanted was
auto-denied, which is the default headless. This wrapper treats that — and an empty
output file, non-JSON output, a populated `error`, and any `denied_actions` — as hard
failures.

Usage:
  agy-review [options] PROMPT_FILE     run the prompt, validate, print the response
  agy-review --check ENVELOPE_JSON     validate an envelope that already exists
  agy-review --restore                 drop --repo grants that killed runs left behind

Options:
  --repo DIR     let the reviewer read the files under DIR (a git work tree) for this run
                 only. See "Repository access" below.
  --model M      model id. Default: gemini-4-argon, at --effort high. Any
                 gemini-3.1-pro id is refused (exit 2), with or without an effort suffix.
  --no-retry     do not step the effort down and retry when agy reports that the answer
                 exceeded the output token limit
  --effort E     low|medium|high. Given explicitly it is ALWAYS forwarded, even to a
                 model that rejects it, so agy's own error surfaces. See below for when
                 the default is applied.
  --out DIR      where to keep out.json and err.txt. Default: a private mkdtemp
                 directory. An existing out.json/err.txt is never overwritten, so two
                 models get two directories and no raw report is lost.
  --timeout T    --print-timeout value (default: 9m)
  --agy PATH     agy binary: a bare name is resolved on PATH, a path is used as given
                 (default: agy, then ~/.local/bin/agy)
  --part-kb N    the size of one part of a multi-turn review, in KB (default 48). See
                 "Multi-turn review" below.
  --single-turn  review the whole prompt in one turn, whatever its size

A single-turn prompt is passed to `agy` as a command-line argument, because `agy -p` does
not read stdin. On a shared machine that makes its full text visible to anyone who can
list processes — do not put secrets in a review bundle. A multi-turn review sends its
turns on stdin instead, and keeps them in in.ndjson beside the output.

Multi-turn review: a prompt holding a git diff (lines starting `diff --git `) bigger than
one part is reviewed as ONE conversation through `--input-format stream-json`. Turn 1
carries the instructions and the whole diff and asks for READY; then one turn per part
names that part's files and asks for the defects located in them, the rest of the diff
being context. Whole file sections are packed in diff order, a section bigger than a
part being a part of its own. The output budget, thinking included, is per turn, so
each part gets all of it while the model still sees every file. Every turn's result
passes the same checks as a single-turn envelope, and the review is printed part by
part under `## Part k/N: <files>`. --repo runs one turn (the reviewer reads what it
needs), and so does --single-turn, a prompt without a git diff, or a diff within one
part. The default since 0.8.0, by the owner's decision on 2026-11-01.

Default model: pinned to gemini-4-argon at --effort high, by the owner's decision on
2026-10-14 (it was gemini-3.8-flash from 2026-09-25). It is the unsuffixed base id so the
effort travels as a flag and can be stepped down on a retry. A newer model is adopted by
editing DEFAULT_MODEL, never automatically. agy offers gemini-4-argon to Google AI Ultra
and eligible Gemini Enterprise accounts only: on any other account the default fails with
agy's "invalid model selection", and `--model gemini-3.8-flash` is the way to keep
reviewing. Because the default sends --effort, that error probably ends `--effort is not
supported for model "gemini-4-argon"` rather than "not recognized": an unknown gemini-* id
answered that way on agy 1.3.4, but it was not reproduced on an account without Argon.
gemini-3.1-pro is banned outright: `--model gemini-3.1-pro-high` (or -low,
or the bare id) exits 2 before anything runs. Which model was used is printed before the
run.

Output token limit: agy answers `status=ERROR`, `response=""`,
`error="...exceeded the output token limit..."` when the answer overruns the model's
output budget — and thinking tokens dominate that budget, so a big prompt at a high
effort fails AFTER the model has already run and been paid for. The documented lever is
to think less, not more: on a 92 KB bundle `--effort high` failed twice where
`--effort medium` succeeded. So this wrapper steps the effort one rung down and runs
again, keeping every attempt's raw report, until the ladder runs out. It only steps an
effort it passes as a FLAG: stepping a suffixed id down would mean inventing a sibling
id that agy need not offer.

To keep the effort instead, give each turn less: the budget is per turn, so a smaller
--part-kb spends less thinking per turn at the same effort. `agy --help` has no budget
flag — only --model and --effort move it — and capping the visible answer buys little,
since a measured run spent 54977 of its 55850 output tokens on thinking. Both routes are
printed when a run cannot be retried.

Effort handling: `agy` rejects --effort for ids that bake the level into the id
(gemini-4-argon-high, claude-opus-5-5-low: "conflicts with --effort=high"), and takes it
on the base id (gemini-4-argon, claude-opus-5-5). Given no explicit --effort, this wrapper
applies the default only to an unsuffixed `gemini-*` or `claude-*` id and omits it
everywhere else, where agy picks the level itself. An explicit --effort is always passed
through, so `agy`'s own error surfaces rather than being silently dropped.

Repository access (--repo DIR): headless agy denies every tool, and the only way to allow
one is an allow-rule in the GLOBAL ~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/settings.json. So for the
length of this run the wrapper adds `read_file(<DIR, resolved>)` there and takes it out
again afterwards — on success, failure, Ctrl-C, SIGTERM and SIGHUP. Verified on agy 1.3.5:
that rule matches whole path components (a sibling `DIR-secret` is denied), resolves
symlinks (a link in DIR pointing outside is denied) and normalises `..`. It refuses to
start when settings.json holds allow-rules of the user's (those are theirs to manage) or is
not plain JSON, and when DIR is /, the home directory or above it, overlaps ~/.gemini
(agy's OAuth token), ~/.ssh, ~/.gnupg or ~/.aws in either direction, or is not in a git
work tree of its own (one rooted at HOME, a dotfiles repo, does not count; the caller's
GIT_* variables are ignored). Every file under DIR is readable, untracked `.env` files
included: the model is told to read only the tracked files listed in the prompt, which is
guidance, not a fence. A model asked to explore a repo runs `find` instead, which is denied
and empties the review, so the wrapper appends the `git ls-files` list to the prompt and
tells the model to read only with its file-viewing tool. While the rule is in place, any
other agy session of this user can use it too. Reading files makes a run slower: give it
a longer --timeout.

Overlapping --repo runs share the grant: the allow-list is always the union of the rules
of the runs still alive (each can then read the others' repos, an accepted trade-off), each
run takes out only its own, and the last one out puts the original bytes back. The
registry `agy-review-grants/` beside settings.json keeps those bytes and one file per run;
an flock on `agy-review.lock` there serialises the edits. A run killed with SIGKILL leaves
its grant; the next --repo run or `agy-review --restore` drops it, telling a dead run from
an unrelated process that reused its pid by the start time `ps` reports. A settings.json
someone else changed while grants were in place is never overwritten: the run stops and
says what to fix by hand.

Print timeout: when --print-timeout ends a turn, agy prints "returning partial output" on
stderr and still reports SUCCESS. That run is refused: whatever the response holds was cut
off mid-turn. agy 1.4.11 and later also set `finished_reason` on such a turn, and any
finished_reason is refused the same way. In a multi-turn run the timeout bounds each
turn, not the session.

Exit codes: 0 review produced, 1 run failed or was incomplete (or the grant could not be
undone), 2 usage or setup error.
"""

import argparse
import contextlib
import fcntl
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import signal
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time

EFFORT_SUFFIXES = ("-high", "-medium", "-low")

# The reviewer when no --model is given, run at DEFAULT_EFFORT. Pinned by the owner's
# decision on 2026-10-14 (gemini-3.8-flash before that); move it to a newer model by
# editing this line.
DEFAULT_MODEL = "gemini-4-argon"
DEFAULT_EFFORT = "high"

# Never run, not even when asked for by name: gemini-3.1-pro in every effort variant.
# Matched case-insensitively on the prefix so gemini-3.1-pro, -high, -low and any later
# suffix are all caught.
BANNED_MODEL_RE = re.compile(r"^gemini-3\.1-pro(?:-|$)", re.IGNORECASE)

# agy's wording when the answer overruns the output budget. Matched as a lowercase
# substring because the rest of the message varies ("Retries remaining: 3").
OUTPUT_LIMIT_MARKER = "output token limit"

# agy's stderr when --print-timeout ends a turn early: "[agy] print timeout after 9m0s
# with turn in progress; returning partial output". Matched lowercase.
PRINT_TIMEOUT_MARKER = "print timeout"

# agy's effort ladder, strongest first.
EFFORT_LADDER = ("high", "medium", "low")

# A 92 KB prompt at --effort high was observed to hit the limit twice, and to succeed at
# medium. Warn a little below that, where the run is worth a heads-up but not a refusal.
LARGE_PROMPT_BYTES = 50 * 1024

# A git diff bigger than one part is reviewed as one conversation: a turn that loads the
# whole diff, then one turn per part. The output budget is per turn, so each part gets the
# whole budget while the model still sees every file. Measured on 2026-11-01 (agy 1.4.12,
# gemini-4-argon high, a 131 KB diff in parts of 47.6, 49.5 and 34.2 KB): the review turns
# thought 18403, 5909 and 7777 tokens, and both planted cross-file defects were found.
DEFAULT_PART_KB = 48
DIFF_HEADER = "diff --git "

# The load turn's expected answer. Anything else is the reviewer's own words, which are
# passed on rather than dropped.
LOAD_ACK = "READY"

# Each review turn repeats this: a tool call is denied headless and fails the review.
TURN_NO_TOOLS = (
    "Answer from the material in this conversation: do NOT call any tools, do NOT run "
    "shell commands, do NOT read files."
)

# agy's global settings, where the --repo grant is written. Relative to $HOME, which is
# where agy itself looks; the tests point HOME at a scratch directory.
SETTINGS_REL = os.path.join(".gemini", "antigravity-cli", "settings.json")
# Beside settings.json: the registry of live grants, and the flock serialising edits.
GRANTS_DIR_NAME = "agy-review-grants"
MUTEX_NAME = "agy-review.lock"
MUTEX_WAIT_SECONDS = 30

# The signals a --repo run turns into an orderly release of its grant.
GRANT_SIGNALS = {signal.SIGINT, signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP}

# The file list travels in the prompt, so a huge tree would crowd out the review itself.
# Past this, review a subdirectory instead.
MAX_REPO_FILES = 3000

# Characters that would let a path end the rule early or smuggle a second one in.
UNSAFE_RULE_CHARS = re.compile(r"[()\x00-\x1f\x7f]")

# The review is UTF-8 whatever the locale says: agy emits UTF-8, and printing it through
# an ASCII stdout would raise UnicodeEncodeError on a perfectly good review.
try:
    sys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8")
    # A diagnosis must always be printable, even when it quotes an undecodable path or a
    # lone surrogate — backslashreplace degrades instead of raising.
    sys.stderr.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8", errors="backslashreplace")
except (AttributeError, ValueError):  # pragma: no cover - very old Python
    pass


def die(msg, code=1):
    print(msg, file=sys.stderr)
    raise SystemExit(code)


def write_response(text):
    """Emit the review unmodified.

    Nothing is stripped, rewrapped or re-indented — leading whitespace is content. The
    single exception is a trailing newline added when the response lacks one, so the
    shell prompt starts on its own line; no other byte is touched.
    """
    try:
        sys.stdout.write(text)
        if not text.endswith("\n"):
            sys.stdout.write("\n")
        sys.stdout.flush()
    except UnicodeEncodeError as e:
        # Surrogates cannot be encoded. Refusing beats emitting a mangled review from a
        # tool whose contract is that the reviewer's words arrive intact.
        die(f"FAILED: the response cannot be encoded for output ({e})")
    except BrokenPipeError:
        # Conventional handling: the reader went away (`| head`), which is not an error
        # of ours. Silence Python's shutdown warning and exit as a killed-by-SIGPIPE
        # process would.
        try:
            devnull = os.open(os.devnull, os.O_WRONLY)
            os.dup2(devnull, sys.stdout.fileno())
        except OSError:
            pass
        raise SystemExit(141)


def takes_effort(model):
    """True when `model` is an id this wrapper sends a separate --effort flag to.

    Verified against agy 1.3.4: a suffixed id conflicts ("conflicts with --effort=high"),
    and the base ids gemini-4-argon, claude-opus-5-5 and claude-sonnet-5-5 all ran with
    --effort. Other families get no flag and agy picks the level, so that a model taking
    none (the claude-*-4-6 ids of agy 1.1.27 refused it outright) still runs.
    """
    if model.endswith(EFFORT_SUFFIXES):
        return False
    return bool(re.match(r"^(gemini|claude)-", model))


def strip_effort_suffix(model):
    """`gemini-3.8-flash-high` -> `gemini-3.8-flash`; anything else unchanged."""
    for suffix in EFFORT_SUFFIXES:
        if model.endswith(suffix):
            return model[: -len(suffix)]
    return model


def lower_effort(effort):
    """The next rung down agy's effort ladder, or None at the bottom or off it."""
    if effort not in EFFORT_LADDER:
        return None
    nxt = EFFORT_LADDER.index(effort) + 1
    return EFFORT_LADDER[nxt] if nxt < len(EFFORT_LADDER) else None


def envelope_error_text(raw):
    """The `error` string in an envelope, or "".

    Never raises: this runs on a failure path, where a parse problem must not replace
    the failure it was called to explain.
    """
    try:
        env = json.loads(raw)
    except (ValueError, RecursionError):
        return ""
    if isinstance(env, dict) and isinstance(env.get("error"), str):
        return env["error"]
    return ""


def hit_output_limit(error_text):
    return OUTPUT_LIMIT_MARKER in error_text.lower()


SPLIT_BY_HAND = (
    "split the bundle and review it in parts — the budget is per run, so half the "
    "diff is half the thinking, at the same effort"
)


def limit_guidance(model, tried, no_retry, keep_lever=SPLIT_BY_HAND):
    """Why the limit failure could not be retried away, and what is actually left.

    Ordered by what the measurements support rather than by what sounds plausible.
    `agy --help` exposes no budget flag at all — only --model and --effort move it — and
    thinking dominates the budget: a successful 92 KB review reported output_tokens
    55850 of which thinking_tokens 54977. So shrinking what one turn reviews is the lever
    that keeps the effort, and capping the visible answer is close to free of effect.
    `keep_lever` says how to shrink it for the run that failed.
    """
    keep = [keep_lever]
    if model is None or not model.lower().startswith("claude-"):
        keep.append(
            "switch to a model with a different budget at the same effort: a claude id "
            "(claude-opus-5-5) — never gemini-3.1-pro, which this wrapper refuses"
        )
    lower = []
    if no_retry:
        nxt = lower_effort(tried[-1]) if tried else None
        if nxt is not None:
            lower.append(
                f"drop --no-retry, or pass --effort {nxt}, to make it think less"
            )
    elif model is not None and not takes_effort(model):
        base = strip_effort_suffix(model)
        if base != model:
            lower.append(
                f"re-run as --model {base} --effort medium — the effort is baked into "
                f"{model}, and stepping a suffixed id down is left to you because the "
                "sibling id need not exist"
            )
        else:
            lower.append(
                f"{model} was run without --effort, so there is no rung to step down "
                "from: pass --effort medium if agy accepts one for it, otherwise only a "
                "different model or a smaller bundle can make it think less"
            )

    lines = [
        "the model ran and its answer — thinking tokens included — overran the output "
        "budget, so re-running it unchanged cannot help. agy has no budget flag: only "
        "--model and --effort move it."
    ]
    if tried:
        lines.append("effort tried: " + ", ".join(tried))
    lines.append("keeping the effort:")
    lines += [f"  - {s}" for s in keep]
    if lower:
        lines.append("or lowering it:")
        lines += [f"  - {s}" for s in lower]
    lines.append(
        "capping the answer buys little: a measured run spent 54977 of its 55850 "
        "output tokens on thinking, so the visible answer is ~2% of the budget."
    )
    return "\n".join(lines)


def read_text(path):
    """Read a text file as UTF-8 regardless of locale — agy emits raw UTF-8 JSON.

    newline="" disables universal-newline translation: a CRLF prompt must reach `agy`
    with its CRLFs intact, or a review OF line-ending behaviour examines different bytes
    than the ones on disk.
    """
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8", newline="") as f:
        return f.read()


def reject_json_constant(name):
    """json.loads accepts NaN/Infinity by default; they are not JSON and not from agy."""
    die(f"FAILED: output contains the non-JSON constant {name}")


def parse_json(raw, what, hint=""):
    """Parse JSON, turning every parser explosion into a diagnosis instead of a traceback.

    One helper for both call sites: the run path used to have its own inline parse that
    lacked the RecursionError guard the --check path had.
    """
    try:
        return json.loads(raw, parse_constant=reject_json_constant)
    except json.JSONDecodeError as e:
        die(f"FAILED: {what} is not JSON ({e}){hint}\nfirst 200 bytes: {raw[:200]!r}")
    except RecursionError:
        die(f"FAILED: {what} is nested too deeply to parse{hint}")
    except ValueError as e:
        # e.g. an integer literal beyond sys.set_int_max_str_digits
        die(f"FAILED: {what} could not be parsed ({e}){hint}")


def envelope_from_text(raw, hint=""):
    """Validate an already-read envelope body."""
    raw = raw.strip()
    if not raw:
        die(f"FAILED: empty output file, the run produced nothing{hint}")

    env = parse_json(raw, "output", hint)
    if not isinstance(env, dict):
        die(f"FAILED: output is not a JSON object, got {type(env).__name__}{hint}")
    return env


def read_envelope(path, err_path=None):
    hint = f" — see {err_path}" if err_path else ""
    try:
        raw = read_text(path)
    except OSError as e:
        die(f"FAILED: cannot read {path}: {e}")
    except UnicodeDecodeError as e:
        die(f"FAILED: {path} is not valid UTF-8 ({e}){hint}")
    return envelope_from_text(raw, hint)


def checked_response(env, hint, repo, where=""):
    """The response of one envelope or turn result, or exit 1 saying why it is unusable.

    `where` names the turn in a multi-turn review ("turn 3 (part 2/4): "), so a failure
    points at the part that had it.
    """
    if env.get("error"):
        die(f"FAILED: {where}status={env.get('status')} error={env['error']}")
    if env.get("status") != "SUCCESS":
        die(f"FAILED: {where}status={env.get('status')}{hint}")
    # agy 1.4.11 and later name why a turn ended early; the field is absent when it ended
    # normally. A print timeout says SUCCESS with a cut-off response (seen on agy 1.4.12),
    # so this is checked before the response is trusted.
    if env.get("finished_reason"):
        die(
            f"FAILED: {where}the turn ended early (finished_reason="
            f"{env['finished_reason']}), so its response is partial{hint}"
        )
    if env.get("denied_actions"):
        die(
            f"FAILED: {where}the run was incomplete — these tools were auto-denied: "
            f"{env['denied_actions']}\n"
            + (
                f"With --repo only file reads under {repo} are allowed: the model "
                "reached for something else. Re-run; if it keeps doing so, put the "
                "material in the prompt instead."
                if repo is not None else
                "Either put the material in the prompt and forbid tool use, or add "
                "allow-rules to ~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/settings.json."
            )
        )

    raw_response = env.get("response")
    if raw_response is not None and not isinstance(raw_response, str):
        die(
            f"FAILED: {where}`response` is {type(raw_response).__name__}, not a "
            f"string{hint}"
        )
    # Strip ONLY to decide emptiness. The response itself is returned untouched — this
    # tool's whole premise is that the reviewer's words reach the user verbatim, and
    # leading whitespace can be the indentation of a markdown code block.
    if not (raw_response or "").strip():
        die(f"FAILED: {where}empty response, nothing was reviewed{hint}")
    return raw_response


def stream_results(raw, hint):
    """The `result` objects of a stream-json run, in order, or exit 1.

    Every line must be a JSON object: stdout carries nothing else in stream-json mode,
    so a stray line means the output is not what agy is known to write. Lines are split
    on "\\n" only — str.splitlines would also break on U+2028, which JSON strings may hold.
    """
    try:
        text = raw.decode("utf-8")
    except UnicodeDecodeError as e:
        die(f"FAILED: the stream output is not valid UTF-8 ({e}){hint}")
    if not text.strip():
        die(f"FAILED: empty output file, the run produced nothing{hint}")
    results = []
    for n, line in enumerate(text.split("\n"), 1):
        if not line.strip():
            continue
        event = parse_json(line, f"stream line {n}", hint)
        if not isinstance(event, dict):
            die(f"FAILED: stream line {n} is not a JSON object{hint}")
        if event.get("event") == "result":
            result = event.get("result")
            if not isinstance(result, dict):
                die(f"FAILED: stream line {n}: `result` is not a JSON object{hint}")
            results.append(result)
    return results


def stream_errors(raw):
    """The `error` strings of every parseable result line, never raising.

    Used on the failure paths (the limit check, a non-zero exit), where a parse problem
    must not replace the failure being explained.
    """
    errors = []
    for line in raw.decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\n"):
        try:
            event = json.loads(line)
        except (ValueError, RecursionError):
            continue
        if isinstance(event, dict) and isinstance(event.get("result"), dict):
            error = event["result"].get("error")
            if isinstance(error, str) and error:
                errors.append(error)
    return errors


def diff_path(section):
    """The path a `diff --git a/X b/Y` section changes (Y), for the turn that names it."""
    first = section.split("\n", 1)[0].rstrip("\r")[len(DIFF_HEADER):]
    return first.rsplit(" b/", 1)[1] if " b/" in first else first


def split_review(prompt, part_bytes):
    """Split a prompt holding a git diff into (instructions, diff, parts), or None.

    `instructions` is everything before the first line that starts with `diff --git `,
    `diff` the rest, both verbatim. `parts` is [(paths, bytes)]: whole file sections packed
    in diff order up to `part_bytes` each, a section bigger than that being a part of its
    own — splitting inside a file would hand the reviewer a hunk without its file. None
    when there is no git diff or it fits in one part: one turn reviews that.
    """
    if prompt.startswith(DIFF_HEADER):
        start = 0
    else:
        start = prompt.find("\n" + DIFF_HEADER)
        if start < 0:
            return None
        start += 1
    instructions, diff = prompt[:start], prompt[start:]
    # Lines inside a section start with " ", "+", "-", "@" or "\", so only a file header
    # starts with the header text.
    bounds = [0] + [m.start() + 1 for m in re.finditer("\n" + re.escape(DIFF_HEADER), diff)]
    sections = [diff[a:b] for a, b in zip(bounds, bounds[1:] + [len(diff)])]

    parts, paths, size = [], [], 0
    for section in sections:
        n = len(section.encode("utf-8", "surrogateescape"))
        if paths and size + n > part_bytes:
            parts.append((paths, size))
            paths, size = [], 0
        paths.append(diff_path(section))
        size += n
    parts.append((paths, size))
    if len(parts) < 2:
        return None
    return instructions, diff, parts


def review_turns(instructions, diff, parts):
    """The user turns of a multi-turn review: load the whole diff, then one per part."""
    n = len(parts)
    gap = "\n" if instructions and not instructions.endswith("\n\n") else ""
    load = (
        f"{instructions}{gap}"
        f"[agy-review] This review runs as one conversation: the diff below is reviewed "
        f"over the next {n} turns, one group of files per turn, and the instructions above "
        f"apply to each of them. Do NOT review anything in this turn. Reply with exactly: "
        f"{LOAD_ACK}\n\n{diff}"
    )
    turns = [load]
    for k, (paths, _) in enumerate(parts, 1):
        turns.append(
            f"Part {k} of {n}. Review the changes to these files in the diff from the "
            "first turn, following the instructions given there:\n"
            + "".join(f"- {p}\n" for p in paths)
            + "\nReport only defects located in these files. Read the rest of the diff as "
            "context: a change in these files that contradicts a change in another file "
            f"counts. {TURN_NO_TOOLS}"
        )
    return turns


def validate(path, err_path=None, env=None, repo=None):
    """Return (response, envelope), or exit non-zero explaining why the run is unusable.

    `env` lets the caller supply an envelope it already read from its own file
    descriptor, so a writable output directory cannot have the file swapped underneath
    between the run and the check.
    """
    if env is None:
        env = read_envelope(path, err_path)
    hint = f" — see {err_path}" if err_path else ""
    response = checked_response(env, hint, repo)

    # `usage` is cosmetic: it is reported, never trusted. A wrong type must not crash the
    # run — and must not discard a review that is otherwise complete either. Warn, drop it.
    usage = env.get("usage")
    if usage is not None and not isinstance(usage, dict):
        print(
            f"warning: `usage` is {type(usage).__name__}, not an object; "
            "token counts unavailable",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        env = dict(env, usage={})
    return response, env


def usable_binary(path):
    return os.path.isfile(path) and os.access(path, os.X_OK)


def find_agy(explicit):
    if explicit:
        # A bare name is resolved through PATH and ONLY through PATH — falling back to a
        # relative path would run ./<name> from the working directory.
        if os.sep not in explicit:
            found = shutil.which(explicit)
            if found:
                return found
            die(f"FAILED: agy binary `{explicit}` not found on PATH", 2)
        expanded = os.path.expanduser(explicit)
        if usable_binary(expanded):
            return expanded
        die(f"FAILED: agy binary not found or not executable at {explicit}", 2)
    found = shutil.which("agy")
    if found:
        return found
    fallback = os.path.expanduser("~/.local/bin/agy")
    if usable_binary(fallback):
        return fallback
    die("FAILED: `agy` not found on PATH or at ~/.local/bin/agy", 2)


def settings_path():
    return os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), SETTINGS_REL)


def digest(data):
    return None if data is None else hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest()


def read_bytes_or_none(path):
    """The file's bytes, or None when it does not exist. A symlink is refused, never
    followed: writing through one would change a file this tool knows nothing about."""
    if os.path.islink(path):
        raise OSError(f"{path} is a symlink; refusing to follow it")
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            return f.read()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None


def atomic_write(path, data, mode):
    """Replace `path` with `data` in one rename, so agy never reads a half-written file."""
    fd, tmp = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix=".agy-review-", dir=os.path.dirname(path))
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "wb") as f:
            f.write(data)
            f.flush()
            os.fchmod(f.fileno(), mode)
            os.fsync(f.fileno())
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except BaseException:
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except OSError:
            pass
        raise


def pid_alive(pid):
    try:
        os.kill(pid, 0)
    except ProcessLookupError:
        return False
    except PermissionError:
        # Another user's process. Grants live in this user's HOME, so it cannot be the
        # run that wrote one: the pid has been reused.
        return False
    return True


def process_start(pid):
    """When `pid` started, as `ps` prints it, or None when that cannot be told."""
    try:
        res = subprocess.run(
            ["ps", "-o", "lstart=", "-p", str(pid)],
            stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, capture_output=True,
            env=dict(os.environ, LC_ALL="C"),
        )
    except OSError:
        return None
    out = res.stdout.decode("ascii", "replace").strip()
    return out if res.returncode == 0 and out else None


def grant_alive(pid, started):
    """True while the run that wrote a grant is still running.

    The start time recorded with the grant tells that run from an unrelated process that
    later got the same pid. When `ps` cannot answer, a live pid counts as alive: keeping
    a rule a little longer is the lesser fault than pulling one from a running review.
    """
    if not pid_alive(pid):
        return False
    if started is None:
        return True
    now = process_start(pid)
    if now is None:
        # ps said nothing: the process may have exited after the check above. Ask again.
        return pid_alive(pid)
    return now == started


class GrantError(Exception):
    """A grant operation that has to stop, with the exit code to stop with."""

    def __init__(self, msg, code):
        super().__init__(msg)
        self.code = code


def config_dir():
    return os.path.dirname(settings_path())


def grants_dir():
    return os.path.join(config_dir(), GRANTS_DIR_NAME)


@contextlib.contextmanager
def settings_mutex():
    """Serialise every edit agy-review makes to settings.json.

    An flock, not a lock directory: the kernel drops it when the holder dies, so a run
    killed mid-edit can never leave it stuck. It is held for the edit only, never for
    the length of a review, which is what lets several --repo runs overlap.
    """
    path = os.path.join(config_dir(), MUTEX_NAME)
    fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT | os.O_NOFOLLOW, 0o600)
    try:
        deadline = time.monotonic() + MUTEX_WAIT_SECONDS
        while True:
            try:
                fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
                break
            except BlockingIOError:
                if time.monotonic() > deadline:
                    raise GrantError(
                        f"FAILED: another agy-review held {path} for "
                        f"{MUTEX_WAIT_SECONDS} s; try again",
                        2,
                    )
                time.sleep(0.1)
        yield
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def check_user_settings(path, current):
    """Refuse a settings.json this tool must not take over: anything but a plain JSON
    object, and any allow-rule already in it, since those are the user's to manage."""
    if current is None:
        return
    try:
        obj = json.loads(current.decode("utf-8"), parse_constant=reject_constant)
    except (ValueError, RecursionError) as e:
        raise GrantError(f"FAILED: {path} is not plain JSON ({e}); not touching it", 2)
    if not isinstance(obj, dict):
        raise GrantError(f"FAILED: {path} is not a JSON object; not touching it", 2)
    perms = obj.get("permissions")
    if perms is not None and not isinstance(perms, dict):
        raise GrantError(f"FAILED: `permissions` in {path} is not an object; not touching it", 2)
    allow = (perms or {}).get("allow")
    if allow is not None and not isinstance(allow, list):
        raise GrantError(f"FAILED: `permissions.allow` in {path} is not a list; not touching it", 2)
    if allow:
        raise GrantError(
            f"FAILED: {path} already has allow-rules: {json.dumps(allow)}. They are yours "
            "to manage, and with them in place a --repo run could do more than read the "
            "repo. Remove them yourself, or review without --repo.",
            2,
        )


def reject_constant(name):
    raise ValueError(f"non-JSON constant {name}")


def render(original, rules):
    """The original settings with `rules` as the whole allow-list."""
    obj = {} if original is None else json.loads(original.decode("utf-8"))
    perms = obj.get("permissions") or {}
    return (json.dumps(dict(obj, permissions=dict(perms, allow=rules)), indent=2) + "\n").encode("utf-8")


def save_private(path, data):
    """Write a registry file at 0600 in one rename."""
    atomic_write(path, data, 0o600)


def update_grants(add_rule=None):
    """Bring settings.json in line with the live grants. Call with the mutex held.

    The registry beside settings.json holds the original bytes (saved by the first
    grant), one grant-<pid>.json per run, and the digests of the settings this tool
    wrote. The allow-list is always exactly the rules of the runs still alive; when the
    last one leaves, the original bytes go back and the registry is removed. Grants of
    dead runs (SIGKILL, a power cut) are dropped on the way.

    A settings.json whose digest is not one this tool wrote is someone else's edit and is
    never overwritten. Returns the rules still granted, or None when there was nothing
    to do because no registry existed.
    """
    path = settings_path()
    gdir = grants_dir()
    state_path = os.path.join(gdir, "state.json")
    orig_path = os.path.join(gdir, "settings.orig")
    if os.path.islink(path):
        raise GrantError(f"FAILED: {path} is a symlink; refusing to follow it", 2)
    current = read_bytes_or_none(path)

    if os.path.islink(gdir):
        raise GrantError(f"FAILED: {gdir} is a symlink; not touching it", 2)
    if os.path.isdir(gdir) and not os.path.exists(state_path):
        # A first grant that died before writing its state never wrote settings.json
        # either (state comes first), so only the half-made registry needs to go.
        shutil.rmtree(gdir)
    if not os.path.isdir(gdir):
        if add_rule is None:
            return None
        check_user_settings(path, current)
        os.mkdir(gdir, 0o700)
        if current is not None:
            save_private(orig_path, current)
        mode = 0o600 if current is None else stat.S_IMODE(os.stat(path).st_mode)
        save_private(state_path, json.dumps({
            "existed": current is not None, "mode": mode, "accepted": [digest(current)],
        }).encode("utf-8"))

    with open(state_path, "rb") as f:
        state = json.loads(f.read().decode("utf-8"))
    original = None
    if state["existed"]:
        with open(orig_path, "rb") as f:
            original = f.read()

    # The original bytes are never someone else's edit: a run killed after putting them
    # back, but before removing the registry, leaves exactly that.
    if digest(current) not in state["accepted"] and current != original:
        live = sorted(n for n in os.listdir(gdir) if n.startswith("grant-"))
        raise GrantError(
            f"FAILED: {path} was changed by someone else while agy-review grants were in "
            "place, so it was not touched. It may still hold the read_file rules of "
            f"{len(live)} run(s). The original is "
            + (f"kept at {orig_path}" if state["existed"] else "no file at all")
            + f". Fix {path} by hand, then delete {gdir}.",
            2 if add_rule is not None else 1,
        )

    rules = set()
    for name in os.listdir(gdir):
        m = re.fullmatch(r"grant-(\d+)\.json", name)
        if not m:
            continue
        pid = int(m.group(1))
        with open(os.path.join(gdir, name), "rb") as f:
            grant = json.loads(f.read().decode("utf-8"))
        # A grant file carrying this process's pid is dropped: on release it is this
        # run's own, and on acquire (which writes its own after this loop) or --restore
        # it was left by a dead run whose pid the OS has since handed to this process.
        if pid == os.getpid() or not grant_alive(pid, grant.get("started")):
            os.unlink(os.path.join(gdir, name))
            continue
        rules.add(grant["rule"])
    if add_rule is not None:
        save_private(
            os.path.join(gdir, f"grant-{os.getpid()}.json"),
            json.dumps({
                "pid": os.getpid(),
                "started": process_start(os.getpid()),
                "rule": add_rule,
            }).encode("utf-8"),
        )
        rules.add(add_rule)

    rules = sorted(rules)
    if rules:
        written = render(original, rules)
        if written != current:
            # Accept both digests while the write is in flight, so a death between the
            # two state updates is not mistaken for someone else's edit.
            state["accepted"] = [digest(current), digest(written)]
            save_private(state_path, json.dumps(state).encode("utf-8"))
            # 0600 while any grant is in place; the original mode returns with the
            # original bytes.
            atomic_write(path, written, 0o600)
            state["accepted"] = [digest(written)]
            save_private(state_path, json.dumps(state).encode("utf-8"))
    else:
        if state["existed"]:
            if current != original:
                atomic_write(path, original, state["mode"])
        elif current is not None:
            os.unlink(path)
        shutil.rmtree(gdir)
    return rules


class RepoGrant:
    """One run's read_file rule in agy's global settings, shared with any other --repo
    runs that overlap it, and its removal.

    main() keeps SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGHUP blocked from the end of the agy run, so the
    release is never cut short; a signal during acquire() is reconciled by its cleanup.
    """

    def __init__(self, repo):
        self.rule = f"read_file({repo})"
        self.path = settings_path()
        self.held = False
        self.registered = False

    def acquire(self):
        if not os.path.isdir(config_dir()):
            die(f"FAILED: {config_dir()} does not exist — run `agy` once in a terminal first", 2)
        cleanup_error = None
        try:
            with settings_mutex():
                # Nothing is written before the mutex is held, so a signal or an error
                # while waiting for it leaves nothing to undo. From here on, signals stay
                # blocked (main() lifts them only while agy runs), and a failure part
                # way through is reconciled under this same lock.
                signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, GRANT_SIGNALS)
                try:
                    update_grants(add_rule=self.rule)
                except GrantError:
                    raise  # every refusal comes before anything is written
                except BaseException:
                    try:
                        update_grants()
                    except BaseException as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - reported below
                        cleanup_error = e
                    raise
                self.held = self.registered = True
        except GrantError as e:
            die(str(e), e.code)
        except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError) as e:
            if cleanup_error is not None:
                die(
                    f"FAILED: could not add the read grant to {self.path} ({e}), nor clean "
                    f"up after it ({cleanup_error}). Inspect it and run "
                    "`agy-review --restore`.",
                    1,
                )
            die(f"FAILED: could not add the read grant to {self.path} ({e})", 2)

    def release(self):
        """Take this run's rule back out. Returns only when that is done."""
        if not self.held:
            return
        self.held = False
        problem, rules = None, None
        try:
            with settings_mutex():
                rules = update_grants()
            if rules is None and self.registered:
                problem = (
                    f"the grant registry {grants_dir()} vanished during the run; "
                    f"{self.path} may still contain `{self.rule}`"
                )
        except GrantError as e:
            problem = str(e).removeprefix("FAILED: ")
        except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError) as e:
            problem = (
                f"could not take `{self.rule}` back out of {self.path} ({e}); inspect it "
                "and run `agy-review --restore`"
            )
        if problem:
            die(f"FAILED: {problem}", 1)
        if rules:
            print(
                f"repo:       grant removed; {len(rules)} rule(s) of other running "
                "reviews stay until they finish",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )


# Directories under HOME that hold credentials. A --repo directory may neither sit inside
# one nor contain one, wherever the symlinks of a dotfiles setup put them.
SECRET_DIRS = (
    (".gemini", "agy's own OAuth token"),
    (".ssh", "SSH keys"),
    (".gnupg", "GPG keys"),
    (".aws", "AWS credentials"),
)


def inside(path, root):
    return path == root or path.startswith(root.rstrip(os.sep) + os.sep)


def git_env():
    """The environment for git, minus GIT_* variables: an exported GIT_DIR or
    GIT_WORK_TREE would make git answer about another repository entirely."""
    return {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if not k.startswith("GIT_")}


def resolve_repo(raw):
    """The directory a --repo run may read, resolved, or a refusal."""
    repo = os.path.realpath(os.path.expanduser(raw))
    if not os.path.isdir(repo):
        die(f"usage: --repo {raw} is not a directory", 2)
    home = os.path.realpath(os.path.expanduser("~"))
    if inside(home, repo):
        die(
            f"usage: --repo {repo} contains the whole home directory, ~/.ssh and agy's own "
            "token included. Name the project directory.",
            2,
        )
    for name, why in SECRET_DIRS:
        secret = os.path.realpath(os.path.join(home, name))
        if inside(repo, secret) or inside(secret, repo):
            die(
                f"usage: --repo {repo} overlaps ~/{name} ({secret}), which holds {why}",
                2,
            )
    if UNSAFE_RULE_CHARS.search(repo):
        die(
            f"usage: --repo {repo!r} contains a parenthesis or a control character, "
            "which cannot be written into an agy rule safely",
            2,
        )
    return repo


def run_git(repo, *args):
    # core.fsmonitor names a program git would run: never from a repo under review.
    try:
        return subprocess.run(
            ["git", "-c", "core.fsmonitor=false", "-C", repo, *args],
            stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, capture_output=True, env=git_env(),
        )
    except OSError as e:
        die(f"FAILED: cannot run git in {repo}: {e}", 2)


def list_repo_files(repo):
    """The tracked files under `repo`, relative to it, as the model will be shown them."""
    top = run_git(repo, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
    # Only the line ending is trimmed: a work tree path may begin or end with a space.
    # An empty answer is no work tree at all; realpath("") would be the caller's cwd.
    toplevel_raw = top.stdout.decode("utf-8", "surrogateescape").rstrip("\r\n")
    if top.returncode != 0 or not toplevel_raw:
        detail = top.stderr.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()
        die(f"usage: --repo {repo} is not in a git work tree ({detail})", 2)
    toplevel = os.path.realpath(toplevel_raw)
    home = os.path.realpath(os.path.expanduser("~"))
    if inside(home, toplevel):
        # A dotfiles repo rooted at HOME makes every directory under it "tracked".
        die(
            f"usage: --repo {repo} is in a git work tree rooted at the home directory "
            f"({toplevel}), not in a project of its own",
            2,
        )
    res = run_git(repo, "ls-files", "-z")
    if res.returncode != 0:
        detail = res.stderr.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()
        die(f"usage: --repo {repo} is not in a git work tree ({detail})", 2)
    files, skipped = [], 0
    for raw in res.stdout.split(b"\0"):
        if not raw:
            continue
        try:
            name = raw.decode("utf-8")
        except UnicodeDecodeError:
            skipped += 1
            continue
        if re.search(r"[\x00-\x1f\x7f]", name):
            skipped += 1  # a newline would split one entry of the list into two
            continue
        files.append(name)
    if skipped:
        print(
            f"warning: {skipped} tracked file(s) under {repo} have names that are not "
            "printable UTF-8 and were left out of the list shown to the model",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
    if not files:
        die(f"usage: --repo {repo} has no tracked files to read", 2)
    if len(files) > MAX_REPO_FILES:
        die(
            f"usage: --repo {repo} has {len(files)} tracked files, more than "
            f"{MAX_REPO_FILES}; the list would crowd out the review. Pass a subdirectory.",
            2,
        )
    return files


def repo_prompt(repo, files, prompt):
    """The user's prompt between the access rules and the file list.

    Measured on agy 1.3.5: told to explore a repo, the model ran `find`, which is denied
    and empties the review; given the list and told to read only, it read files with
    view_file and finished, three runs out of three.
    """
    rules = (
        f"Repository access for this review: you may read files under {repo} with your "
        "file-viewing tool (read_file / view_file), and nothing else. Every other tool is "
        "denied in this run, and a single denied call discards the whole review: do NOT "
        "run shell commands, do NOT search, do NOT list directories. Use the list of "
        "tracked files at the end of this prompt instead of exploring. Paths in it are "
        f"relative to {repo}: join them with that directory to get the absolute path to "
        "read. Read what you need for context; you do not have to read everything.\n\n"
    )
    listing = f"\n\nTracked files under {repo} ({len(files)}):\n" + "\n".join(files) + "\n"
    return rules + prompt + listing


def finish_turns(raw, turns, parts, err_path, summary):
    """Check every turn of a multi-turn review and print the review part by part.

    Exits 1 unless every turn answered and passed the same checks as a single-turn
    envelope. Each part's review is printed verbatim under a heading naming its files; a
    load turn that answered with more than READY is printed first, since those are the
    reviewer's words too.
    """
    hint = f" — see {err_path}"
    results = stream_results(raw, hint)
    if len(results) != len(turns):
        die(
            f"FAILED: {len(turns)} turns were sent and {len(results)} answered, so the "
            f"review is incomplete{hint}"
        )
    n = len(parts)
    wheres = ["turn 1 (load): "] + [f"turn {k + 1} (part {k}/{n}): " for k in range(1, n + 1)]
    responses = [checked_response(r, hint, None, w) for r, w in zip(results, wheres)]

    chunks = []
    if responses[0].strip().rstrip(".").upper() != LOAD_ACK:
        print(
            f"warning: the load turn answered with more than {LOAD_ACK}; that answer is "
            "printed first, verbatim",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        chunks.append(
            f"## Load turn (the reviewer answered instead of acknowledging)\n\n{responses[0]}"
        )
    for k, ((paths, _), response) in enumerate(zip(parts, responses[1:]), 1):
        chunks.append(f"## Part {k}/{n}: {', '.join(paths)}\n\n{response}")

    last = results[-1]
    usage = last.get("usage") if isinstance(last.get("usage"), dict) else {}
    print(
        f"{summary} parts={n} turns={len(results)} "
        f"conversation={last.get('conversation_id')} tokens={usage.get('total_tokens')}",
        file=sys.stderr,
    )
    write_response("\n".join(c if c.endswith("\n") else c + "\n" for c in chunks))
    return 0


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(add_help=False)
    ap.add_argument("target", nargs="?")
    ap.add_argument("--check", metavar="ENVELOPE_JSON")
    # Defaults stay None so "not given" is distinguishable from "given the default",
    # which is what lets --check reject run options instead of ignoring them.
    ap.add_argument("--model", default=None)
    ap.add_argument("--effort", default=None)
    ap.add_argument("--out")
    ap.add_argument("--timeout", default=None)
    ap.add_argument("--agy")
    ap.add_argument("--no-retry", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--repo", default=None)
    ap.add_argument("--single-turn", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--part-kb", default=None)
    ap.add_argument("--restore", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("-h", "--help", action="store_true")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    if args.help:
        print(__doc__)
        return 0

    # An empty value is a mistake, never a request for the default — otherwise
    # `--check "$MAYBE_UNSET"` quietly turns a validation into a real, billed review.
    for flag, value in (
        ("--check", args.check), ("--model", args.model), ("--effort", args.effort),
        ("--out", args.out), ("--timeout", args.timeout), ("--agy", args.agy),
        ("--repo", args.repo), ("--part-kb", args.part_kb),
    ):
        if value is not None and not value.strip():
            die(f"usage: {flag} was given an empty value", 2)

    part_kb = DEFAULT_PART_KB
    if args.part_kb is not None:
        if not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9]+", args.part_kb.strip()) or int(args.part_kb) < 1:
            die(f"usage: --part-kb takes a whole number of KB, 1 or more, got {args.part_kb!r}", 2)
        part_kb = int(args.part_kb)
        if args.single_turn:
            die("usage: --part-kb sizes the parts of a multi-turn review; --single-turn has none", 2)
        if args.repo is not None:
            die("usage: --part-kb sizes the parts of a multi-turn review; --repo runs one turn", 2)

    if args.model is not None and BANNED_MODEL_RE.match(args.model.strip()):
        die(
            f"usage: --model {args.model} is banned — gemini-3.1-pro is never used. "
            f"Omit --model for the default ({DEFAULT_MODEL}, effort {DEFAULT_EFFORT}), "
            "or pass a claude id for a second opinion.",
            2,
        )

    if args.restore:
        given = [
            name
            for name, value in (
                ("PROMPT_FILE", args.target), ("--check", args.check),
                ("--out", args.out), ("--agy", args.agy), ("--effort", args.effort),
                ("--model", args.model), ("--timeout", args.timeout),
                ("--no-retry", args.no_retry or None), ("--repo", args.repo),
                ("--single-turn", args.single_turn or None), ("--part-kb", args.part_kb),
            )
            if value is not None
        ]
        if given:
            die(f"usage: --restore takes no other options, got: {', '.join(given)}", 2)
        if not os.path.isdir(config_dir()):
            print(f"nothing to restore: {config_dir()} does not exist", file=sys.stderr)
            return 0
        try:
            with settings_mutex():
                rules = update_grants()
        except GrantError as e:
            die(str(e), 1)
        except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError) as e:
            die(f"FAILED: could not restore {settings_path()} ({e})", 1)
        if rules is None:
            print("nothing to restore: no --repo grant is outstanding", file=sys.stderr)
        elif rules:
            print(
                f"grants of dead runs removed; {len(rules)} rule(s) of running reviews "
                f"stay until they finish: {', '.join(rules)}",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
        else:
            print(f"restored {settings_path()}; no --repo grant is left", file=sys.stderr)
        return 0

    if args.check is not None:
        # --check validates an existing envelope; nothing about a run applies to it.
        # Silently ignoring run options would hide a mistyped command.
        conflicting = [
            name
            for name, value in (
                ("PROMPT_FILE", args.target),
                ("--out", args.out),
                ("--agy", args.agy),
                ("--effort", args.effort),
                ("--model", args.model),
                ("--timeout", args.timeout),
                ("--no-retry", args.no_retry or None),
                ("--repo", args.repo),
                ("--single-turn", args.single_turn or None),
                ("--part-kb", args.part_kb),
            )
            if value is not None
        ]
        if conflicting:
            die(
                f"usage: --check takes no run options, got: {', '.join(conflicting)}", 2
            )
        response, _ = validate(args.check)
        write_response(response)
        return 0

    timeout = args.timeout if args.timeout is not None else "9m"

    if not args.target:
        die("usage: agy-review [options] PROMPT_FILE | --check ENVELOPE_JSON", 2)
    if not os.path.isfile(args.target):
        die(f"FAILED: prompt file not found: {args.target}", 2)

    try:
        prompt = read_text(args.target)
    except OSError as e:
        die(f"FAILED: cannot read prompt file {args.target}: {e}", 2)
    except UnicodeDecodeError as e:
        die(f"FAILED: prompt file {args.target} is not valid UTF-8 ({e})", 2)
    if not prompt.strip():
        die("FAILED: prompt file is empty", 2)
    if "\0" in prompt:
        # exec() cannot carry a NUL; without this the failure is a ValueError traceback
        # from deep inside subprocess.
        die(f"FAILED: prompt file {args.target} contains a NUL byte", 2)

    repo = None
    if args.repo is not None:
        repo = resolve_repo(args.repo)
        prompt = repo_prompt(repo, list_repo_files(repo), prompt)

    # A git diff bigger than one part is reviewed as one conversation, one turn per part.
    # --repo keeps one turn: its prompt is the repo listing wrapped around the user's text,
    # and the reviewer reads what it needs itself.
    split = None
    if repo is None and not args.single_turn:
        split = split_review(prompt, part_kb * 1024)
    turns = None if split is None else review_turns(*split)
    parts = None if split is None else split[2]

    # What keeps the effort when an answer overruns the output budget: a smaller turn.
    many_files = split_review(prompt, 1) is not None
    if turns is not None:
        keep_lever = (
            f"smaller parts: --part-kb {max(1, part_kb // 2)} — each turn has its own "
            "output budget, so half the part is about half the thinking, at the same effort"
        )
    elif args.single_turn and many_files:
        keep_lever = (
            "drop --single-turn: a git diff bigger than one part is then reviewed as one "
            "conversation, a turn per part, each turn with its own output budget"
        )
    elif repo is None and many_files:
        keep_lever = (
            f"--part-kb {max(1, len(prompt.encode('utf-8', 'surrogateescape')) // 2048)}, "
            "half the diff: it is then reviewed as one conversation, a turn per part, "
            "each turn with its own output budget"
        )
    else:
        keep_lever = SPLIT_BY_HAND

    binary = find_agy(args.agy)

    # Default to a private 0700 directory. A predictable shared path under /tmp would
    # expose the review — prompt and response both — to any local user, and invites
    # pre-created-file and symlink attacks.
    try:
        if args.out:
            os.makedirs(args.out, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
            out_dir = args.out
            # makedirs' mode applies only when it creates the directory. An existing
            # 0755 --out would leave the report world-readable, so say so rather than
            # silently changing permissions on a directory the caller owns.
            mode = stat.S_IMODE(os.stat(out_dir).st_mode)
            if mode & (stat.S_IRWXG | stat.S_IRWXO):
                print(
                    f"warning: {out_dir} is mode {mode:04o}; other local users can list "
                    "it. The report files themselves are created 0600.",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )
        else:
            out_dir = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="agy-review-")
    except OSError as e:
        die(f"FAILED: cannot use output directory {args.out or '(temp)'}: {e}", 2)

    def report_paths(attempt):
        """(out, in, err) of one attempt; `in` is None for a single-turn run. The first
        attempt keeps the plain names."""
        sfx = "" if attempt == 1 else f"-{attempt}"
        err = os.path.join(out_dir, f"err{sfx}.txt")
        if turns is None:
            return os.path.join(out_dir, f"out{sfx}.json"), None, err
        return (
            os.path.join(out_dir, f"out{sfx}.ndjson"),
            os.path.join(out_dir, f"in{sfx}.ndjson"),
            err,
        )

    out_path, in_path, err_path = report_paths(1)

    # Refuse to clobber a previous run's raw report: two models written to one --out
    # would destroy the first, and an attacker-planted symlink would be followed.
    for path in (out_path, in_path, err_path):
        if path is not None and os.path.lexists(path):
            die(
                f"FAILED: {path} already exists — pick a different --out so an earlier "
                "run's raw report is not overwritten",
                2,
            )

    if args.model is not None:
        model, model_source = args.model, "explicit --model"
    else:
        model, model_source = DEFAULT_MODEL, "default"

    def private_create(path):
        """Create at exactly 0600, never following a symlink.

        O_CREAT|O_EXCL refuses an existing path, symlink included. The mode passed to
        os.open is still masked by the umask — which can only remove bits, so the file
        is never more permissive than 0600, but under a umask like 0200 it would come
        out 0400. fchmod pins it.
        """
        fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL, 0o600)
        try:
            os.fchmod(fd, 0o600)
        except OSError:  # some filesystems do not support it; 0600-or-tighter still holds
            pass
        return os.fdopen(fd, "w+b")

    # The effort this run owns. An explicit --effort is forwarded verbatim; the default
    # is applied only where agy accepts it. Only an effort that travels in the FLAG can
    # be stepped down on a retry — stepping a suffixed id would mean inventing a sibling
    # id that agy need not offer.
    effort = args.effort if args.effort is not None else (
        DEFAULT_EFFORT if takes_effort(model) else None
    )
    steppable = takes_effort(model) and not args.no_retry

    prompt_bytes = len(prompt.encode("utf-8", "surrogateescape"))
    if turns is None and prompt_bytes >= LARGE_PROMPT_BYTES and effort == "high":
        print(
            f"warning: {prompt_bytes // 1024} KB of prompt at --effort high. Thinking "
            "tokens count against the output budget: a 92 KB bundle exceeded the output "
            "token limit at high effort and succeeded at medium. A limit failure here is "
            "retried a rung lower.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
    elif turns is not None and effort == "high":
        k, (_, size) = max(enumerate(parts, 1), key=lambda kp: kp[1][1])
        if size >= LARGE_PROMPT_BYTES:
            print(
                f"warning: part {k} is {size // 1024} KB at --effort high. Its turn has "
                "the whole output budget, but thinking tokens count against it; a limit "
                "failure here is retried a rung lower.",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )

    # Each turn of a multi-turn review is one NDJSON message on agy's stdin. The shape
    # is not in agy's --help: it was found by probing agy 1.4.12, whose errors name the
    # fields ("missing the \"event\" field", "\"user\" message is missing the
    # \"message\" field", "has no content").
    turn_input = None if turns is None else "".join(
        json.dumps({"event": "user", "message": {"content": t}}) + "\n" for t in turns
    ).encode("ascii")

    tried = []
    grant = None
    if repo is not None:
        # SIGTERM and SIGHUP would otherwise end Python without running `finally`, and
        # leave the grant in agy's global settings. The handler blocks further signals
        # and raises, which unwinds through subprocess.call (killing agy) into the
        # `finally` below. Once agy has run, signals stay blocked, so none can land
        # inside that `finally` and cut the release short. Before that, a signal during
        # acquire() unwinds through acquire's own cleanup.
        def unwind(signum, _frame):
            signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, GRANT_SIGNALS)
            raise SystemExit(128 + signum)
        for s in GRANT_SIGNALS:
            signal.signal(s, unwind)
        grant = RepoGrant(repo)
    try:
        if grant is not None:
            grant.acquire()
            print(
                f"repo:       {grant.rule} added to {grant.path} for this run",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
        while True:
            attempt = len(tried) + 1
            if attempt > 1:
                # Each attempt keeps its own raw report: the failed one is the evidence that
                # the retry was warranted, and overwriting it would destroy that.
                out_path, in_path, err_path = report_paths(attempt)
                for path in (out_path, in_path, err_path):
                    if path is not None and os.path.lexists(path):
                        die(
                            f"FAILED: {path} already exists — pick a different --out so an "
                            "earlier run's raw report is not overwritten",
                            2,
                        )

            if turns is None:
                cmd = [
                    binary,
                    "-p", prompt,
                    "--model", model,
                    "--disable-slash-commands",
                    "--output-format", "json",
                    "--print-timeout", timeout,
                ]
            else:
                # No -p: the turns arrive on stdin. --print-timeout bounds each turn, not
                # the session (agy 1.4.12: three turns of ~3 s each all finished under a
                # 6 s timeout, 9 s in total).
                cmd = [
                    binary,
                    "--model", model,
                    "--disable-slash-commands",
                    "--input-format", "stream-json",
                    "--output-format", "stream-json",
                    "--print-timeout", timeout,
                ]
            if effort is not None:
                cmd += ["--effort", effort]

            # Announce the paths BEFORE running: a pre-exec failure (a bad shebang in the
            # target, say) would otherwise leave an implicitly created temp directory nobody
            # can find. The model goes here too, so a fallback is visible even when the run
            # then fails — every finding has to be reported with the model that produced it.
            print(
                f"model:      {model} ({model_source})"
                + (f", effort {effort}" if effort is not None else ""),
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            if turns is not None:
                print(
                    f"turns:      {len(turns)}: one loads the whole diff, {len(parts)} "
                    f"review it in parts of up to {part_kb} KB",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )
                print(f"raw input:  {in_path}", file=sys.stderr)
            print(f"raw output: {out_path}", file=sys.stderr)
            print(f"stderr:     {err_path}", file=sys.stderr)

            envelope_bytes = None
            try:
                with contextlib.ExitStack() as files:
                    out_f = files.enter_context(private_create(out_path))
                    err_f = files.enter_context(private_create(err_path))
                    stdin = subprocess.DEVNULL
                    if turns is not None:
                        # Kept beside the output: what each turn asked is part of the record.
                        stdin = files.enter_context(private_create(in_path))
                        stdin.write(turn_input)
                        stdin.flush()
                        stdin.seek(0)
                    # agy inherits the signal mask, so it is lifted for exactly the
                    # child's lifetime: Ctrl-C reaches agy, and a signal that arrived
                    # while blocked is delivered here, inside the `try`.
                    if grant is not None:
                        signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_UNBLOCK, GRANT_SIGNALS)
                    try:
                        rc = subprocess.call(
                            cmd, stdin=stdin, stdout=out_f, stderr=err_f
                        )
                    finally:
                        if grant is not None:
                            signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, GRANT_SIGNALS)
                    # Read the envelope back through the SAME descriptor we handed the child.
                    # Re-opening by pathname would let anyone who can write the output
                    # directory unlink out.json and drop a forged SUCCESS envelope in its
                    # place between the run and the check.
                    out_f.flush()
                    out_f.seek(0)
                    envelope_bytes = out_f.read()
                    # The same for stderr, which the print-timeout check reads.
                    err_f.flush()
                    err_f.seek(0)
                    stderr_bytes = err_f.read()
            except OSError as e:
                die(f"FAILED: could not run {binary}: {e}", 2)
            except ValueError as e:
                die(f"FAILED: could not run {binary}: {e}", 2)

            tried.append(effort if effort is not None else "n/a")

            # The limit error arrives with either exit code, so it is inspected before the
            # exit-code branch below: a retryable failure must not be reported as a dead end.
            # Only THIS error is retried — every other one means running again changes
            # nothing, and would just bill a second time for the same answer.
            if turns is None:
                limit_hit = hit_output_limit(
                    envelope_error_text(envelope_bytes.decode("utf-8", "replace"))
                )
            else:
                # A stream-json session warns and goes on to the next turn after a failed
                # one, so the limit can sit in any turn's result.
                limit_hit = any(hit_output_limit(e) for e in stream_errors(envelope_bytes))
            if limit_hit:
                nxt = lower_effort(effort) if steppable else None
                if nxt is not None:
                    print(
                        f"warning: attempt {attempt} at --effort {effort} exceeded the "
                        f"output token limit; retrying at --effort {nxt}. The failed "
                        f"attempt is kept at {out_path}.",
                        file=sys.stderr,
                    )
                    effort = nxt
                    continue
                die(
                    f"FAILED: {limit_guidance(model, tried, args.no_retry, keep_lever)}\n"
                    f"— see {err_path}"
                )
            break
    finally:
        if grant is not None:
            grant.release()
            # The grant is gone; a signal held back while it was being taken out is
            # delivered now and ends the run with 128 + signum, as it was meant to.
            signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_UNBLOCK, GRANT_SIGNALS)

    # A non-zero exit is reported with its code before any envelope complaint, so the
    # real failure is never masked by "empty output file".
    if rc != 0:
        # Best-effort extra detail. The exit code is the finding here, so any problem
        # reading or parsing the envelope is swallowed rather than replacing it — but it
        # must not become a traceback either, hence the same guards as the --check path.
        detail = ""
        if turns is not None:
            errors = stream_errors(envelope_bytes or b"")
            detail = f": {errors[0]}" if errors else ""
        else:
            try:
                raw = read_text(out_path).strip()
                if raw:
                    parsed = json.loads(raw)
                    if isinstance(parsed, dict) and parsed.get("error"):
                        detail = f": {parsed['error']}"
            except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError, ValueError, RecursionError):
                pass
        die(f"FAILED: agy exited {rc}{detail} — see {err_path}")

    # Seen on agy 1.3.5 (a --repo review at gemini-4-argon, effort high): at the print
    # timeout agy says "returning partial output" on stderr and still reports SUCCESS.
    # Whatever the response holds then is a review cut off mid-turn, never a whole one.
    # Read through the descriptor agy wrote to, never by pathname: a file swapped or
    # removed in a writable --out must not hide the marker.
    if PRINT_TIMEOUT_MARKER in stderr_bytes.decode("utf-8", "replace").lower():
        die(
            f"FAILED: agy hit --print-timeout {timeout} with the turn still in progress "
            "and returned partial output, so there is no complete review. Re-run with a "
            f"longer --timeout (a --repo review reads files first and takes longer) — see "
            f"{err_path}"
        )

    if turns is not None:
        return finish_turns(
            envelope_bytes, turns, parts, err_path,
            f"model={model} effort={tried[-1]} attempts={len(tried)}",
        )

    try:
        envelope_text = envelope_bytes.decode("utf-8")
    except UnicodeDecodeError as e:
        die(f"FAILED: {out_path} is not valid UTF-8 ({e}) — see {err_path}")
    env = envelope_from_text(envelope_text, f" — see {err_path}")
    response, env = validate(out_path, err_path, env=env, repo=repo)

    usage = env.get("usage") or {}
    print(
        f"model={model} effort={tried[-1]} attempts={len(tried)} "
        f"conversation={env.get('conversation_id')} "
        f"tokens={usage.get('total_tokens')} turns={env.get('num_turns')}",
        file=sys.stderr,
    )
    write_response(response)
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
